TFTP: 
  |--> UDP TRANSPORT
        SMALL, VERY SIMPLE COMMUNICATION
        NO TERMINAL COMMS
        INSECURE

FTP:
  |--> 2 DIFFERENT TCP CONNECTIONS
  |      CONTROL (21)/ DATA(20)
  |      AUTH IN CLEAR TEXT
  |      HAS DIRECTORY SERVICES
  |      ANONYMOUS LOGIN
  |
  |---> FTPS: SECURE
          |--> ADDS SSL/TLS ENCRPTION
                INTERACTIVE TERMINAL ACCESS 
                EXPLICIT MODE:20/21
                  |--> OPTION FOR ENCRYPTION
                IMPLICIT MODE:989/990
                  |--> ENCRPTION ASSUMED


SFTP:
  |--> TCP (22)
        ADDS FTP LIKE SERVICE TO SSH 
        INTERACTIVE

SCP:
  |--> TCP(22)
        SYMMETRIC AND ASYMMETRIC ENCRYPTION
        AUTH THROUGH SIGN IN
        NON-INTERACTIVE
      > OPTIONS
          |--> -V verbose
                -P alternate port
                -R recursively copy an entire directory
                -3 3-way copy
      > SYNTAX  
          |--> scp student@172.16.82.106:secretstuff.txt /home/student
                #Download a file from a remote directory to a local directory

                scp secretstuff.txt student@172.16.82.106:/home/student
                #Upload a file to a remote directory from a local directory

                scp -3 student@172.16.82.106:/home/student/secretstuff.txt student@172.16.82.112:/home/student
                #Copy a file from a remote host to a separate remote host

                scp -r folder/ student@172.16.82.106:
                #Recursive upload of a folder to remote

                scp -r student@172.16.82.106:folder/ .
                #Recursive download of a folder from remote


      > SCP Syntax w/ alternate SSHD
          |--> scp -P 1111 student@172.16.82.106:secretstuff.txt .
                #Download a file from a remote directory to a local directory

                scp -P 1111 secretstuff.txt student@172.16.82.106:
                #Upload a file to a remote directory from a local directory

      > SCP Syntax through a tunnel
          |--> ssh student@172.16.82.106 -L 1111:localhost:22 -NT
                #Create a local port forward to target device

                scp -P 1111 student@localhost:secretstuff.txt /home/student
                #Download a file from a remote directory to a local directory

                scp -P 1111 secretstuff.txt student@localhost:/home/student
                #Upload a file to a remote directory from a local directory

      > SCP Syntax through a Dynamic Port forward
          |--> ssh student@172.16.82.106 -D 9050 -NT
                #Create a Dynamic Port Forward to target device

                proxychains scp student@localhost:secretstuff.txt .
                #Download a file from a remote directory to a local directory

                proxychains scp secretstuff.txt student@localhost:
                #Upload a file to a remote directory from a local directory


  NETCAT:
    |--> READ AND WRITES DATA ACROSS NETWORK, TCP CONNECTION
    |      INBOUND AND OUBOUND CONNECTION, TCP/UDP TOOR FROM ANY PORT 
    |      SEND/RECIVE DATA 
    |      PORT SCANNING 
    |
    |
    |--> NETCAT: Client to Listener file transfer
            |--> nc -lvp 9001 > newfile.txt
                  #Listener (receive file)

                  nc 172.16.82.106 9001 < file.txt
                  #Client (sends file)


        > NETCAT: Listener to Client file transfer
              |--> nc -lvp 9001 < file.txt
                    #Listener (sends file)

                    nc 172.16.82.106 9001 > newfile.txt
                    #Client (receive file)

        > File Transfer with /dev/tcp
            |--> nc -lvp 1111 > devtcpfile.txt
                  #On the receiving box

                cat secret.txt > /dev/tcp/10.10.0.40/1111
                #On the sending box
                USEFUL FOR A HOST THAT DOENT HAVE NETCAT 


